{
  "image_name": "4d792892a66c5db6b7a2bb75b7f7a0ce.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sulfur metabolism, mitochondrial copper handling, and antioxidant defense intersect to determine three forms of regulated cell death: ferroptosis, cuproptosis, and disulfidoptosis. At the cell membrane, cystine enters through the transporter SLC7A11 and is converted to cysteine, which supports synthesis of GSH. GSH then feeds the activity of GPX4, a central brake on ferroptosis. The diagram shows that hydrogen peroxide can suppress GSH, while copper promotes GPX4 loss both directly and through autophagic degradation of GPX4, thereby favoring ferroptosis.\n\nA second branch emphasizes that under glucose starvation, imported cystine can drive disulfide stress, leading to disulfidoptosis. The mitochondrion is depicted as another hub: GSH appears to enter mitochondria via SLC25A39, where it restrains copper. Copper accumulation, together with mitochondrial factors including FDX1 and LIAS and Fe–S processes, promotes cuproptosis. Mitochondrial ROS and the CoQ10H2 system at the membrane are also linked to ferroptotic control.\n\nOverall, the image presents a network in which cystine uptake, GSH availability, GPX4 integrity, and mitochondrial copper metabolism collectively decide whether cells are protected or pushed toward distinct death pathways.",
